shows that every integer satisfying the three conditions indeed yields an infinite sequence (it reaches a fixed point after at most \\(t\\) steps).  \n\nCombining the necessity and sufficiency we obtain the complete description of all admissible \\(a_{1}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\text{(sum of the three largest proper divisors of }N).\n\\]\n\nFor a positive integer \\(N\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}=1<d_{2}<d_{3}<\\dots<d_{k}=N\n\\]\n\nthe complete list of its positive divisors.  \nIf \\(N\\) has at least five divisors (i.e. at least three proper divisors besides \\(1\\)), then the three largest proper divisors are  \n\n\\[\nd_{k-1}=N/d_{2},\\qquad d_{k-2}=N/d_{3},\\qquad d_{k-3}=N/d_{4}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}+\\frac1{d_{4}}\\Bigr)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhenever \\(N\\) possesses at least five divisors.  \nIf \\(N\\) has exactly four divisors, then \\(N\\) is either a product of two distinct primes,\n\\(N=pq\\;(p<q)\\), or a cube of a prime, \\(N=p^{3}\\).  In these two cases\n\n\\[\nf(pq)=p+q+1,\\qquad f(p^{3})=p^{2}+p+1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Behaviour of \\(f\\) according to the three smallest proper divisors\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na=d_{2},\\;b=d_{3},\\;c=d_{4}\\qquad (2\\le a<b<c).\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(f(N)\\) is governed by the sum  \n\n\\[\nS(a,b,c)=\\frac1a+\\frac1b+\\frac1c .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(N\\) is odd*, then \\(a\\ge3,\\;b\\ge5,\\;c\\ge7\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nS(a,b,c)\\le\\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac17=\\frac{71}{105}<1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(f(N)<N\\).  The sequence therefore strictly decreases; an odd integer has no\nfixed point, and a decreasing sequence of positive integers must eventually reach a\nprime (or a square of a prime), contradicting the hypothesis that the sequence is\ndefined for all \\(n\\).  Hence **every admissible \\(a_{1}\\) is even**.\n\n*If \\(N\\) is even but \\(3\\nmid N\\)*, then \\(a=2\\) and \\(b\\ge4\\).  Moreover \\(c\\ge5\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nS(a,b,c)\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n\\]\n\nso again \\(f(N)<N\\) and the same argument forces a prime to appear.  Hence **\\(3\\mid a_{1}\\)**.\n\nConsequently any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) is a multiple of \\(6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(v_{2}(N)=1\\)\n\nWrite \\(N=2\\cdot3^{k}\\,m\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\) odd.  \nIf \\(5\\mid N\\) then the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,5\\) and  \n\n\\[\nS=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15=\\frac{31}{30}>1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(f(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N\\) is odd; the sequence then becomes odd and, as shown\nabove, terminates.  Hence **\\(5\\nmid N\\)**.\n\nIf \\(5\\nmid N\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); now  \n\n\\[\nS=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16=1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(f(N)=N\\).  Thus every integer of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L=2\\cdot3^{k}\\,m\\qquad(k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nis a **fixed point** of the recurrence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(v_{2}(N)\\ge2\\)\n\nAssume \\(12\\mid N\\); then the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\), and\n\n\\[\nS=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14=\\frac{13}{12}>1,\n\\qquad\\boxed{f(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nN=2^{\\,2t+1}\\,3^{\\,k}\\,m\\qquad(t\\ge0,\\;k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd}).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(5\\mid m\\) the same argument as in \u00a72 shows that after the exponent of \\(2\\)\nhas been reduced to \\(1\\) the three smallest divisors become \\(2,3,5\\) and the\nsequence terminates.  Hence **\\(5\\nmid m\\)** is necessary.\n\nApply (4) repeatedly.  After the first step the exponent of \\(2\\) drops by \\(2\\)\nand the exponent of \\(3\\) drops by \\(1\\); after \\(i\\) steps we obtain\n\n\\[\na_{i+1}=13^{\\,i}\\,2^{\\,2(t-i)+1}\\,3^{\\,k-i}\\,m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le t). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor the iteration to stay within the admissible set we must keep the factor\n\\(3\\) present until the exponent of \\(2\\) has become \\(1\\); this requires\n\n\\[\nk-i\\ge1\\quad\\text{for }i=t\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k\\ge t+1.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWhen (6) holds, (5) with \\(i=t\\) gives\n\n\\[\na_{t+1}=13^{\\,t}\\,2\\cdot3^{\\,k-t}\\,m,\n\\]\n\nwhich is of the form (3); consequently \\(a_{t+1}\\) is a fixed point and the\nsequence becomes constant thereafter.  Thus every integer satisfying\n\n\\[\nv_{2}(a_{1})=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0),\\qquad v_{3}(a_{1})\\ge t+1,\\qquad5\\nmid a_{1}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nproduces an infinite sequence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of the conditions\n\nLet the sequence be infinite.  From \u00a71 we already have \\(2\\mid a_{1}\\) and\n\\(3\\mid a_{1}\\); write \\(a_{1}=2^{e}\\,3^{k}\\,m\\) with \\(e\\ge1,\\;k\\ge1\\) and\n\\(m\\) odd.\n\n*If \\(e\\) were even*, after \\(e/2\\) applications of (4) the exponent of \\(2\\)\nwould become \\(0\\), producing an odd term and a contradiction.  Hence **\\(e\\) is\nodd**, say \\(e=2t+1\\).\n\nEach use of (4) reduces the exponent of \\(3\\) by exactly \\(1\\); therefore after\n\\(t\\) steps the exponent of \\(2\\) is \\(1\\) and the exponent of \\(3\\) equals\n\\(k-t\\).  To avoid the appearance of the divisor \\(5\\) (which would force the\nnext term to be odd) we must have \\(k-t\\ge1\\), i.e. **\\(k\\ge t+1\\)**.\nFinally, if \\(5\\mid m\\) then after the exponent of \\(2\\) has become \\(1\\) the\nthree smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,5\\); the next term is odd and the\nsequence cannot continue.  Hence **\\(5\\nmid m\\)**.\n\nThus any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) necessarily satisfies (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe conditions (7) are both necessary and sufficient.  In terms of the\n2\u2011adic and 3\u2011adic valuations they read\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_{2}(a_{1})\\text{ is odd},\\qquad \n       v_{3}(a_{1})\\ge\\frac{v_{2}(a_{1})+1}{2},\\qquad \n       5\\nmid a_{1}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, all admissible starting values are precisely the integers of the\nform  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2^{\\,2t+1}\\,3^{\\,k}\\,m\\qquad\n       (t\\ge0,\\;k\\ge t+1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} .\n\\]\n\nFor such an \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence reaches a fixed point after at most \\(t\\)\nsteps and thereafter remains constant; for any other positive integer the\nsequence inevitably encounters a prime or a square of a prime, so it cannot be\ncontinued indefinitely.  This completes the determination of all possible values\nof \\(a_{1}\\)."
