"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}\\text{ is any even integer divisible by }3\\text{ but not by }4\\text{ or }5\\;}\n\\]\n\nequivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\ge 1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;\\gcd(m,30)=1)} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Recurrence in terms of the three smallest proper divisors.**  \n   If \\(p<q<r\\) are the three smallest proper divisors of a positive integer \\(N\\) (excluding the divisor \\(1\\)), then the three largest proper divisors are \\(\\frac{N}{p},\\frac{N}{q},\\frac{N}{r}\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1}=T(N):=N\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Classification by the 2\u2011adic valuation \\(v_{2}(N)\\).**  \n\n   *If \\(v_{2}(N)\\ge2\\):* \\(p=2,\\;q=4\\) and \\(r\\ge5\\); then \\(\\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r<1\\) so \\(T(N)<N\\).  \n\n   *If \\(v_{2}(N)=1\\) (i.e. \\(N=2M\\) with \\(M\\) odd):*  \n\n   - If \\(3\\nmid M\\) then \\(p=2,\\;q\\ge5,\\;r\\ge2q\\); again \\(\\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r<1\\) and \\(T(N)<N\\).  \n\n   - If \\(3\\mid M\\) and \\(5\\nmid M\\) then the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\). Consequently  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16=1,\n     \\]\n\n     whence \\(T(N)=N\\).  \n\n   - If \\(3\\mid M\\) and \\(5\\mid M\\) then the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,5\\); we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     T(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15\\Bigr)=\\frac{31}{30}N,\n     \\]\n\n     which is an odd integer (because \\(30\\mid N\\)).  \n\n   *If \\(N\\) is odd:* the smallest proper divisor is at least \\(3\\); the sum of the three reciprocals is \\(<1\\), so \\(T(N)<N\\).\n\n3. **Fixed\u2011point condition.**  \n   For the sequence to be infinite every term must satisfy \\(T(N)=N\\); otherwise the term strictly decreases (or jumps to an odd term that then decreases), and a strictly decreasing infinite sequence of positive integers cannot exist because eventually a term would have fewer than three proper divisors.  \n\n   Equation \\(T(N)=N\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=1,\n   \\]\n\n   with distinct integers \\(2\\le p<q<r\\). The only solution in distinct positive integers is  \n\n   \\[\n   (p,q,r)=(2,3,6),\n   \\]\n\n   because solving \\(1/p+1/q+1/r=1\\) yields \\(p=2\\) and \\((q-2)(r-2)=4\\), giving the unique triple \\((2,3,6)\\).\n\n4. **Consequences for \\(N\\).**  \n   The triple \\((2,3,6)\\) must be the three smallest proper divisors \\(>1\\) of \\(N\\). Hence  \n\n   * \\(2\\) is the smallest prime factor \u21d2 \\(N\\) is even.  \n   * \\(3\\) must also divide \\(N\\).  \n   * No divisor \\(4\\) or \\(5\\) may appear before \\(6\\); therefore \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).  \n\n   These conditions are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{2}(N)=1,\\qquad 3\\mid N,\\qquad 4\\nmid N,\\qquad 5\\nmid N.\n   \\]\n\n   Writing the odd part of \\(N\\) as \\(3^{k}m\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(m,30)=1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   N=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;\\gcd(m,30)=1).\n   \\]\n\n   For any such \\(N\\) the three largest proper divisors are \\(\\frac{N}{2},\\frac{N}{3},\\frac{N}{6}\\); their sum equals \\(N\\), so the sequence is constant and therefore infinite.\n\n**Conclusion:** The admissible initial values are precisely the even multiples of \\(3\\) that are not divisible by \\(4\\) or \\(5\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\cdot m\\;(k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;\\gcd(m,30)=1)\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nd_{0}=1<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots<d_{t}=N\n\\]\n\nbe the divisors of a positive integer \\(N\\).  \nThe proper divisors are \\(d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{t-1}\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\np=d_{1},\\qquad q=d_{2},\\qquad r=d_{3}\n\\]\n\nthe three smallest proper divisors **greater than \\(1\\)** (i.e. the three smallest elements of \\(\\{d_{i}\\mid i\\ge1\\}\\)).  \nBecause divisors occur in complementary pairs \\(d\\leftrightarrow N/d\\), the three largest proper divisors are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{p},\\qquad\\frac{N}{q},\\qquad\\frac{N}{r}.\n\\]\n\nHence the recurrence of the problem can be written as  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{n+1}=T(a_{n})\\;,\\qquad \nT(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r\\Bigr)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p<q<r\\) are defined as above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Behaviour according to the 2\u2011adic valuation  \n\nWrite \\(v_{2}(N)=\\) the exponent of the prime \\(2\\) in the factorisation of \\(N\\).\n\n* **Case \\(v_{2}(N)\\ge2\\).**  \n  Then \\(p=2,\\;q=4\\) and \\(r\\ge5\\). Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n  \\]\n\n  and by (1)  \n\n  \\[\n  T(N)<N. \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n* **Case \\(v_{2}(N)=1\\).**  \n  Write \\(N=2M\\) with \\(M\\) odd.\n\n  *If \\(3\\nmid M\\).*  \n  The smallest odd divisor of \\(M\\) is at least \\(5\\); call it \\(s\\).  \n  Then the three smallest proper divisors of \\(N\\) are \\(2,s,2s\\). Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=\\frac12+\\frac1s+\\frac1{2s}\n  =\\frac{s+3}{2s}<1\\qquad(s\\ge5),\n  \\]\n\n  so \\(T(N)<N\\).  \n\n  *If \\(3\\mid M\\) and \\(5\\nmid M\\).*  \n  The three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\). Thus  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16=1,\n  \\]\n\n  whence  \n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{T(N)=N}. \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  *If \\(3\\mid M\\) and \\(5\\mid M\\).*  \n  The three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,5\\). Then  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15=\\frac{31}{30}>1,\n  \\]\n\n  and because \\(30\\mid N\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  T(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N,\n  \\]\n\n  which is an odd integer. In particular \\(T(N)>N\\) and the next term is odd.\n\n* **Case \\(N\\) odd.**  \n  Here \\(p\\ge3\\); with three distinct integers \\(\\ge3\\) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r<\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac13=1,\n  \\]\n\n  so \\(T(N)<N\\).  \n\nSummarising, for every \\(N\\) we have exactly one of the following:\n\n| Type of \\(N\\) | Relation between \\(T(N)\\) and \\(N\\) |\n|---------------|--------------------------------------|\n| odd | \\(T(N)<N\\) |\n| even, \\(v_{2}\\ge2\\) | \\(T(N)<N\\) |\n| even, \\(v_{2}=1,\\;3\\nmid N\\) | \\(T(N)<N\\) |\n| even, \\(v_{2}=1,\\;3\\mid N,\\;5\\nmid N\\) | \\(T(N)=N\\) |\n| even, \\(v_{2}=1,\\;3\\mid N,\\;5\\mid N\\) | \\(T(N)>N\\) and \\(T(N)\\) odd |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Fixed\u2011point condition  \n\nIf the infinite sequence \\((a_{n})\\) exists, every term must have at least three proper divisors.  \nWhenever a term falls into any of the first four rows of the table (i.e. \\(T(N)<N\\)), the next term is strictly smaller. A strictly decreasing infinite sequence of positive integers cannot exist, because eventually a term would be \\(\\le5\\), which possesses at most two proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\nIf a term falls into the fifth row (\\(v_{2}=1,\\;3\\mid N,\\;5\\mid N\\)), the next term is odd; the subsequent term then belongs to the \u201codd\u201d row and is smaller. Hence the sequence again cannot be infinite.  \n\nTherefore **the only way an infinite sequence can exist is that every term satisfies the third row**, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nT(N)=N.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Solving \\(T(N)=N\\)\n\nFrom (1) the equality \\(T(N)=N\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(2\\le p<q<r\\) are distinct integers (the three smallest proper divisors \\(>1\\) of \\(N\\)).  \n\n**Lemma.** The only solution in distinct positive integers of (4) is \\((p,q,r)=(2,3,6)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(p\\ge3\\) then \\(\\frac1p\\le\\frac13\\), so the left\u2011hand side is at most \\(\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15<1\\), impossible. Hence \\(p=2\\).  \nEquation (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac1q+\\frac1r=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\frac1q+\\frac1r=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(qr\\) gives \\(2(q+r)=qr\\), i.e. \\((q-2)(r-2)=4\\).  \nSince \\(q>2\\) and \\(r>q\\), the only factorisation of \\(4\\) with \\(q-2<r-2\\) is \\(1\\cdot4\\). Hence \\(q-2=1,\\;r-2=4\\), giving \\(q=3,\\;r=6\\). \u220e  \n\nThus the three smallest proper divisors of any admissible \\(N\\) must be exactly \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Characterisation of admissible \\(N\\)\n\nThe condition \u201cthe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\)\u201d translates into:\n\n* \\(2\\) is the smallest prime factor \u21d2 \\(N\\) is even.  \n* \\(3\\) also divides \\(N\\).  \n* No divisor \\(4\\) or \\(5\\) may appear before \\(6\\); consequently \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\nThese requirements are precisely  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}(N)=1,\\qquad 3\\mid N,\\qquad 4\\nmid N,\\qquad 5\\nmid N.\n\\]\n\nWriting the odd part of \\(N\\) as \\(3^{k}m\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(m,30)=1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;\\gcd(m,30)=1)}.\n\\]\n\nFor any such \\(N\\) the three largest proper divisors are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{2},\\;\\frac{N}{3},\\;\\frac{N}{6},\n\\]\n\nand their sum equals \\(N\\); therefore the sequence is the constant infinite sequence  \n\n\\[\nN,N,N,\\dots .\n\\]\n\nConversely, any integer not of this form eventually produces a term that is either smaller (strict decrease) or odd (then smaller), and the decreasing process must terminate with a number \\(\\le5\\) that lacks three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence no other initial value can generate an infinite sequence satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer ###  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;\\gcd(m,30)=1)\\;}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(a_{1}\\) is any even integer divisible by \\(3\\) but not divisible by \\(4\\) or \\(5\\). For each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant, and for any other \\(a_{1}\\) the required infinite sequence cannot exist."
