d m}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy \\(f(N)=N\\).  For such an \\(N\\) the sequence is constant:\n\\(a_{1}=a_{2}=a_{3}= \\dots =N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No other infinite sequences\n\nAssume that \\(N\\) is admissible (has at least three proper divisors) but does\n*not* satisfy (3).  We show that the iteration must eventually leave the set\nof admissible numbers.\n\n---\n\n**(i)  The smallest odd divisor \\(p\\ge 5\\).**  \n\nThen \\(d_{2}=2,\\;d_{3}=p\\) and \\(d_{4}\\ge p^{2}\\).  From (1),\n\n\\[\nf(N)\\le\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{p}+\\frac{N}{p^{2}}\n\\le N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac1{25}\\Bigr)<N .\n\\]\n\nThus the sequence strictly decreases.  A decreasing sequence of positive\nintegers cannot stay forever inside the set of admissible numbers; after a\nfinite number of steps it reaches a prime or a square of a prime, contradicting\nthe requirement that every term have at least three proper divisors.\n\n---\n\n**(ii)  \\(4\\mid N\\) (i.e. \\(N\\) contains the factor \\(2^{2}\\)).**  \n\nNow \\(d_{2}=2,\\;d_{3}=4\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nf(N)\\le\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{4}+\\frac{N}{d_{4}}\n\\le\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{4}+\\frac{N}{4}=N .\n\\]\n\nEquality would force \\(d_{4}=4\\), which would mean that \\(3\\nmid N\\); then\n\\(N\\) is a power of \\(2\\).  For \\(N=2^{k}\\;(k\\ge3)\\),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=2^{k-1}+2^{k-2}+2^{k-3}=2^{k-3}(7)<2^{k}=N .\n\\]\n\nAgain the sequence decreases and eventually leaves the admissible set.\n\n---\n\n**(iii)  \\(5\\mid N\\) while \\(3\\mid N\\) and \\(4\\nmid N\\).**  \n\nHere the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,5\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{5}=N\\cdot\\frac{31}{30}>N .\n\\]\n\nThe next term now has smallest odd divisor \\(5\\); we fall under case (i), and\nthe sequence subsequently decreases to a non\u2011admissible number.\n\n---\n\nIn every possible deviation from (3) the iteration either strictly decreases\nor first increases and then enters a decreasing phase, and in both situations\nthe process cannot continue indefinitely while preserving the condition\n\u201cat least three proper divisors\u201d.  Therefore no infinite sequence can start\nfrom a number outside the set (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe only initial values that allow the infinite sequence described in the\nproblem are precisely the numbers\n\n\\[\na_{1}=6m\\qquad\\text{with }m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) we have \\(a_{2}=a_{1}\\) and the sequence remains\nconstant, satisfying all the required conditions.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=6m\\text{ where }m\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid m\\;}\n\\]"
