}}+\\frac1{d_{4}}=\\frac12 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d_{3}<d_{4}\\) are positive integers, the only solution of (3) is  \n\n\\[\n(d_{3},d_{4})=(3,6).\n\\]\n\nThus the three smallest proper divisors must be \\(2,3,6\\).  \nThis occurs precisely when\n\n* \\(N\\) is divisible by \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) (so \\(6\\mid N\\));\n* \\(N\\) is **not** divisible by any integer between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\), i.e. \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\nWriting \\(N=6m\\), the condition \\(4\\nmid N\\) forces \\(m\\) to be odd, and \\(5\\nmid N\\) is equivalent to \\(5\\nmid m\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N=6m\\text{ with }m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConversely, for any integer of the form (4) the three smallest proper divisors are exactly \\(2,3,6\\); therefore the three largest proper divisors are \\(N/2,N/3,N/6\\), and\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac N2+\\frac N3+\\frac N6=N .\n\\]\n\nThus every \\(N\\) of the shape (4) is a fixed point of the transformation \\(f\\).  \nFor such an \\(N\\) the sequence \\((a_{n})\\) is constant, and each term possesses the proper divisors \\(1,2,3\\), i.e. at least three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Any other initial value leads to a decreasing sequence\n\nAssume \\(N\\) has at least three proper divisors but does **not** satisfy (4).  \nLet \\(p\\) denote the smallest odd divisor of \\(N\\) (if \\(N\\) is odd, then \\(p=d_{2}\\); if \\(N\\) is even, then \\(p=d_{3}\\)).  \nWe distinguish three exhaustive cases.\n\n---\n\n**Case A.** \\(p\\ge5\\).\n\n*If \\(N\\) is odd.*  \nThen \\(d_{2}=p\\ge5,\\;d_{3}\\ge p+2,\\;d_{4}\\ge p+4\\). Using (1),\n\n\\[\nf(N)\\le N\\!\\left(\\frac1{5}+\\frac1{7}+\\frac1{9}\\right)<N .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(N\\) is even.*  \nThen \\(d_{2}=2,\\;d_{3}=p\\ge5\\).\n\n- If \\(4\\mid N\\), then \\(d_{4}=4\\) and  \n\n  \\[\n  f(N)\\le N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1p+\\frac14\\right)\n  \\le N\\!\\left(\\frac34+\\frac15\\right)<N .\n  \\]\n\n- If \\(4\\nmid N\\), then \\(d_{4}\\ge2p\\) (since \\(2p\\mid N\\)) and  \n\n  \\[\n  f(N)\\le N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1p+\\frac1{2p}\\right)\n  =N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac{3}{2p}\\right)\n  \\le N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac{3}{10}\\right)=0.8N<N .\n  \\]\n\nIn all subcases \\(f(N)<N\\); the sequence becomes strictly decreasing after the next step.\n\n---\n\n**Case B.** \\(4\\mid N\\) but \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\nHere \\(d_{2}=2,\\;d_{3}=4\\) and \\(d_{4}\\ge4\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nf(N)\\le N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac14\\right)=N .\n\\]\n\nEquality forces \\(d_{4}=4\\), which implies that \\(N\\) has no divisor between \\(4\\) and \\(N\\); consequently \\(N=2^{k}\\) for some \\(k\\ge3\\). For such \\(N\\),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac N2+\\frac N4+\\frac N8=\\frac{7N}{8}<N .\n\\]\n\nThus again \\(f(N)<N\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case C.** \\(5\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N,\\;4\\nmid N\\).\n\nThe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,5\\); from (1),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\!\\left(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15\\right)=\\frac{31}{30}N>N .\n\\]\n\nThe next term \\(N'=\\frac{31}{30}N\\) is not divisible by \\(3\\) (since \\(\\gcd(31,3)=1\\)) and its smallest odd divisor is at least \\(5\\); therefore \\(N'\\) falls under Case A, and the following term satisfies \\(f(N')<N'\\). Hence after at most two iterations the sequence becomes strictly decreasing.\n\n---\n\nIn every situation where \\(N\\) does not satisfy (4) the sequence becomes strictly decreasing after at most two steps. A strictly decreasing infinite sequence of positive integers cannot exist; it must eventually reach a number that has fewer than three proper divisors (a prime or a square of a prime), contradicting the requirement that all terms possess at least three proper divisors. Consequently no infinite sequence can start from such an \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the argument\n\nThe only admissible infinite sequences are those that start at a fixed point of \\(f\\).  \nBy the analysis of Section 1, the fixed points are exactly the integers described by (4).  \nFor each such integer the sequence remains constant, thereby satisfying the condition that every term has at least three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer\n\nAll possible values of the first term \\(a_{1}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6m\\text{ where }m\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid m}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above. Verify that every statement contributes directly to the final, coherent mathematical argument."
