"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe only possible values of \\(a_{1}\\) are the integers whose prime factorisation is  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot p_{1}^{e_{1}}\\cdots p_{r}^{e_{r}},\n\\qquad \n\\alpha\\ge 1,\\; r\\ge 0,\n\\]\n\nwhere each odd prime \\(p_{i}\\) satisfies \\(p_{i}\\ge 7\\) (in particular \\(5\\nmid a_{1}\\)).  \nEquivalently,  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\quad(\\alpha\\ge 1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\; \\text{all prime divisors of }m\\ge 7)} .\n\\]\n\nFor every such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant: \\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\).  \nFor any other starting integer the sequence eventually produces a term with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(N\\) let its proper divisors be ordered  \n   \\[\n   d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots <d_{k}<N .\n   \\]\n   The three largest proper divisors are \\(N/d_{1},\\,N/d_{2},\\,N/d_{3}\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N):=a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers \\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}\\) are precisely the three *smallest* divisors of \\(N\\) that are larger than \\(1\\).\n\n2. **Fixed points.**  \n   If the sequence is infinite, it must either be eventually constant or else contain a term with fewer than three proper divisors.  \n   Solving \\(f(N)=N\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(d_{1}\\ge 2\\), (2) forces \\(d_{1}=2\\).  \n   Writing \\(x=d_{2},y=d_{3}\\) with \\(2\\le x\\le y\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1x+\\frac1y=\\frac12\\Longrightarrow (x-2)(y-2)=4 .\n   \\]\n\n   The only solution with distinct divisors is \\((x,y)=(3,6)\\).  \n   Hence a fixed point must have its three smallest divisors equal to \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n3. **Characterisation of the fixed points.**  \n   The condition \u201cthe three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,6\\)\u201d is equivalent to  \n\n   * the exponent of \\(2\\) in the factorisation of \\(N\\) is exactly \\(1\\);  \n   * \\(3\\mid N\\);  \n   * \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   N=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m,\n   \\qquad \\alpha\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) is odd, \\(\\gcd(m,6)=1\\) and every prime divisor of \\(m\\) is at least \\(7\\).  \n   For such an \\(N\\) the three smallest divisors are indeed \\(2,3,6\\), so (2) holds and \\(f(N)=N\\).  \n   Hence the sequence is constant and satisfies the required condition for all \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Exclusion of all other starting values.**  \n   *If the smallest divisor of \\(N\\) is \\(\\ge 3\\)*, then from (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\n        < N\\cdot\\frac{3}{3}=N .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the sequence is strictly decreasing until an even term appears.\n\n   *When an even term appears* we distinguish three possibilities for its three smallest divisors:\n\n   - **\\(2,3,4\\).**  \n     Then \\(f(N)=N\\cdot\\frac{13}{12}>N\\).  \n     Since \\(N\\) is a multiple of \\(\\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,4)=12\\), we can write \\(N=12k\\) and obtain \\(f(N)=13k\\).  \n     The integer \\(13k\\) is either prime (if \\(k=1\\)) or has at most two proper divisors (if \\(k\\) is a power of \\(13\\)). In every case the next term fails the hypothesis.\n\n   - **\\(2,3,5\\)** (or any third divisor \\(\\ge5\\) while \\(4\\mid N\\) is absent).  \n     Then \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{5}>1\\), so again \\(f(N)>N\\) and the same argument as above yields a term with at most two proper divisors.\n\n   - **\\(2,3,6\\).**  \n     This is precisely the fixed\u2011point situation already described.\n\n   - **\\(2,4,\\dots\\)** (i.e. \\(3\\nmid N\\)).  \n     Here \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{4}+\\frac1{d_{3}}<1\\); the sequence decreases.  \n     Repeating the argument forces the appearance of a term whose smallest divisor is \\(\\ge3\\); as shown earlier the sequence then strictly decreases until an even term is reached, and the previous cases apply. Consequently a term with fewer than three proper divisors inevitably occurs.\n\n   Hence any starting integer that does **not** satisfy the fixed\u2011point condition inevitably produces a term lacking at least three proper divisors, contradicting the problem statement.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible initial values are exactly the fixed points described in step\u202f3.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1},a_{2},\\dots\n\\]\n\nbe the infinite sequence defined in the problem.  \nFor a positive integer \\(N\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n1<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots <d_{k}<N\n\\]\n\nthe proper divisors of \\(N\\) written in increasing order.  \nThe three *largest* proper divisors of \\(N\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{d_{1}},\\qquad \\frac{N}{d_{2}},\\qquad \\frac{N}{d_{3}},\n\\]\n\nhence, by the definition of the sequence,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(N):=a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Fixed points of \\(f\\)\n\nIf the sequence is infinite, either it is eventually constant or some term has fewer than three proper divisors, which is forbidden.  \nThus a permissible infinite sequence must eventually satisfy \\(f(N)=N\\).  \nEquation \\(f(N)=N\\) together with (1) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d_{1}\\ge 2\\), (2) forces \\(d_{1}=2\\).  \nWrite \\(x=d_{2}\\) and \\(y=d_{3}\\) with \\(2\\le x\\le y\\).  \nThen (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1x+\\frac1y=\\frac12\\Longrightarrow 2(x+y)=xy\\Longrightarrow (x-2)(y-2)=4.\n\\]\n\nThe positive integer solutions of \\((x-2)(y-2)=4\\) are \\((x-2,y-2)=(1,4),(2,2),(4,1)\\).  \nThe pair \\((2,2)\\) would give \\(x=y=4\\), contradicting the distinctness of the three smallest divisors.  \nHence \\((x,y)=(3,6)\\).  \n\nConsequently a fixed point must have its three smallest proper divisors equal to \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Description of the fixed points  \n\nThe condition \u201cthe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\)\u201d is equivalent to\n\n* the exponent of \\(2\\) in the prime factorisation of \\(N\\) is exactly \\(1\\);\n* \\(3\\mid N\\);\n* \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\nIndeed, if the exponent of \\(2\\) were at least \\(2\\) then \\(4\\) would be a divisor smaller than \\(6\\); if \\(5\\mid N\\) then \\(5\\) would appear before \\(6\\); and if \\(3\\nmid N\\) then the second smallest divisor would be at least \\(4\\).\n\nHence every fixed point can be written as  \n\n\\[\nN=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is odd, \\(\\gcd(m,6)=1\\), and no prime divisor of \\(m\\) equals \\(5\\).  \nEquivalently, all prime factors of \\(m\\) are at least \\(7\\).  \n\nFor such an \\(N\\) the three smallest proper divisors are indeed \\(2,3,6\\); therefore (2) holds and \\(f(N)=N\\).  \nThus the sequence is constant:\n\n\\[\na_{1}=a_{2}=a_{3}=\\dots =N .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. No other starting values are possible  \n\nAssume that \\(a_{1}=N\\) does **not** satisfy the description above.  \nWe show that the sequence must eventually contain a term with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n*If the smallest proper divisor of \\(N\\) is \\(\\ge 3\\).*  \nThen \\(d_{1}\\ge 3\\) and from (1)\n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\n      \\le N\\cdot\\frac{3}{3}=N,\n\\]\n\nwith strict inequality because the three divisors cannot all equal \\(3\\).  \nHence the sequence is strictly decreasing until an even term appears.\n\n*Now let an even term \\(E\\) appear.*  \nWrite the three smallest proper divisors of \\(E\\) as \\(2\\le d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\\).  \nFour possibilities arise.\n\n1. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,4\\).**  \n   Then \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{4}= \\frac{13}{12}>1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   f(E)=E\\cdot\\frac{13}{12}>E .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(E\\) is a multiple of \\(\\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,4)=12\\), write \\(E=12k\\); then  \n\n   \\[\n   f(E)=13k .\n   \\]\n\n   The integer \\(13k\\) is either prime (if \\(k=1\\)) or has at most two proper divisors (if \\(k\\) is a power of \\(13\\)). In any case the next term fails to have three proper divisors.\n\n2. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,5\\)** (or any third divisor \\(\\ge5\\) while \\(4\\nmid E\\)).  \n   Here \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{5}= \\frac{31}{30}>1\\); the same computation as in (1) shows that the next term is of the form \\((\\text{prime})\\times(\\text{integer})\\) and again possesses at most two proper divisors.\n\n3. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,6\\).**  \n   This is precisely the fixed\u2011point situation already described; the sequence becomes constant and satisfies the requirement.\n\n4. **\\(d_{1}=2\\) but \\(3\\nmid E\\).**  \n   Then the three smallest divisors are \\(2,4,d_{3}\\) (or \\(2,4,5\\) etc.).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1{2}+\\frac1{4}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(f(E)<E\\). The sequence therefore decreases again, and the argument repeats until an even term of type (1)\u2013(3) is reached, which forces a failure unless the term is of type (3).\n\nThus any initial integer that is not of the form described in \u00a72 inevitably leads to a term with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis of an infinite sequence.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Final answer  \n\nThe admissible initial values are exactly the fixed points found in \u00a72:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\quad(\\alpha\\ge 1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\\text{all prime divisors of }m\\ge 7)} .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(a_{1}\\) is any integer whose prime factorisation contains the prime \\(2\\) to the first power, contains the prime \\(3\\) at least once, and contains no prime factor smaller than \\(7\\) other than \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).  \nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant, \\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\), and every term has at least three proper divisors.  \n\nNo other starting integer can generate an infinite sequence satisfying the given condition."
