"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe only possible values of \\(a_{1}\\) are the integers whose prime factorisation is  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m ,\n\\qquad \n\\alpha\\ge 1,\\; m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\n\\text{every prime divisor of }m\\ge 7 .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\;( \\alpha\\ge 1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\n\\text{all prime factors of }m\\ge 7)} .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant: \\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\).  \nNo other starting integer can generate an infinite sequence satisfying the hypothesis.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Write the three largest proper divisors of \\(N\\) as \\(N/d_{1},N/d_{2},N/d_{3}\\), where \\(d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\\) are the three smallest proper divisors **greater than\u202f1**.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N):=a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. A perpetual sequence must eventually satisfy \\(f(N)=N\\); otherwise a term would have fewer than three proper divisors.  \n   Solving \\(f(N)=N\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(d_{1}\\ge2\\), (2) forces \\(d_{1}=2\\).  \n   Setting \\(x=d_{2},y=d_{3}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1x+\\frac1y=\\frac12\\Longrightarrow (x-2)(y-2)=4 .\n   \\]\n\n   The only solution with distinct divisors is \\((x,y)=(3,6)\\).  \n   Thus a fixed point must have its three smallest proper divisors equal to \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n3. \u201cThe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\)\u201d is equivalent to  \n\n   * the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(N\\) is exactly \\(1\\) (otherwise \\(4\\mid N\\));  \n   * \\(3\\mid N\\);  \n   * \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\).\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   N=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m,\n   \\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(m\\) is odd, \\(\\gcd(m,6)=1\\), and every prime divisor of \\(m\\) is at least \\(7\\).  \n   For such an \\(N\\) the three largest proper divisors are \\(N/2,N/3,N/6\\); their sum equals \\(N\\).  \n   Hence the sequence is constant and each term possesses at least the proper divisors \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n4. **Excluding all other starting values.**  \n\n   *If the smallest proper divisor of a term is \\(\\ge3\\)*, then from (1)  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\n        <N\\cdot\\frac{3}{3}=N ,\n   \\]\n\n   so the sequence strictly decreases until an even term appears.\n\n   *Let an even term \\(E\\) appear.*  \n   Denote its three smallest proper divisors (greater than\u202f1) by \\(d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\\).  \n   Four possibilities occur.\n\n   1. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,4\\).**  \n      Then \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{4}=13/12>1\\) and  \n\n      \\[\n      f(E)=E\\cdot\\frac{13}{12}=13\\cdot\\frac{E}{12}=13k .\n      \\]\n\n      The integer \\(13k\\) has at most two proper divisors greater than\u202f1 unless \\(k\\) itself has at least two distinct proper divisors.  \n      In any case the next term is either a prime (if \\(k=1\\)) or of the form \\(13p\\) with \\(p\\) prime, whose three largest proper divisors sum to a number having fewer than three proper divisors. Hence the hypothesis fails.\n\n   2. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,5\\)** (i.e. \\(5\\mid E\\) but \\(4\\nmid E\\)).  \n      Here \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{5}=31/30>1\\) and  \n\n      \\[\n      f(E)=E\\cdot\\frac{31}{30}=31\\cdot\\frac{E}{30}=31k .\n      \\]\n\n      The same reasoning as in (1) shows that the subsequent term cannot have three proper divisors, contradicting the requirement.\n\n   3. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,6\\).**  \n      This is precisely the fixed\u2011point situation described in \u00a73; the sequence becomes constant and satisfies the condition.\n\n   4. **\\(d_{1}=2\\) but \\(3\\nmid E\\).**  \n      Then the three smallest proper divisors are at most \\(2,4,5\\); consequently  \n\n      \\[\n      \\frac1{2}+\\frac1{4}+\\frac1{5}<1 ,\n      \\]\n\n      so \\(f(E)<E\\). The sequence decreases again, eventually reaching a term whose smallest proper divisor is \\(\\ge3\\), and we return to the first case. Hence a term with fewer than three proper divisors must appear unless we are in case\u202f3.\n\n   Therefore any initial integer that does **not** have the form \\(2\\cdot3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\) (with the restrictions on \\(m\\)) inevitably produces a term lacking at least three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis of an infinite sequence.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible initial values are exactly the fixed points found in \u00a73.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1},a_{2},\\dots\n\\]\n\nbe the infinite sequence defined in the problem.  \nFor a positive integer \\(N\\) write its proper divisors (excluding \\(N\\) itself) as  \n\n\\[\n1<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots <d_{k}<N .\n\\]\n\nThe three *largest* proper divisors of \\(N\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{d_{1}},\\qquad \\frac{N}{d_{2}},\\qquad \\frac{N}{d_{3}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}\\) are the three smallest proper divisors **greater than\u202f1**.  \nHence, by the definition of the sequence,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(N):=a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Fixed points  \n\nIf the sequence is infinite, it must eventually satisfy \\(f(N)=N\\); otherwise a term would have fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nEquation \\(f(N)=N\\) together with (1) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}=1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d_{1}\\ge2\\), (2) forces \\(d_{1}=2\\).  \nPut \\(x=d_{2},\\;y=d_{3}\\;(2\\le x\\le y)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1x+\\frac1y=\\frac12\n\\Longrightarrow 2(x+y)=xy\n\\Longrightarrow (x-2)(y-2)=4 .\n\\]\n\nThe positive integer solutions of \\((x-2)(y-2)=4\\) are \\((1,4),(2,2),(4,1)\\).  \nThe pair \\((2,2)\\) would give \\(x=y=4\\), contradicting the distinctness of the three smallest proper divisors.  \nThus \\((x,y)=(3,6)\\).  \n\nConsequently a fixed point must have its three smallest proper divisors equal to \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Description of the fixed points  \n\nThe condition \u201cthe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\)\u201d is equivalent to  \n\n* the exponent of \\(2\\) in the prime factorisation of \\(N\\) is exactly \\(1\\) (otherwise \\(4\\mid N\\) would be a smaller divisor than \\(6\\));\n* \\(3\\mid N\\);\n* \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(4\\) or \\(5\\) would appear before \\(6\\)).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nN=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is odd, \\(\\gcd(m,6)=1\\), and every prime divisor of \\(m\\) is at least \\(7\\).  \n\nFor such an \\(N\\) the three largest proper divisors are \\(N/2,\\;N/3,\\;N/6\\); their sum equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{6}=N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16\\Bigr)=N .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(f(N)=N\\) and the sequence is constant:\n\\[\na_{1}=a_{2}=a_{3}=\\dots =N .\n\\]\nEach term possesses at least the proper divisors \\(2,3,6\\), so the hypothesis is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. No other starting values are possible  \n\nAssume that \\(a_{1}=N\\) does **not** have the form described above.  \nWe prove that the sequence must eventually contain a term with fewer than three proper divisors.\n\n*If the smallest proper divisor of a term is \\(\\ge3\\).*  \nThen \\(d_{1}\\ge3\\) in (1) and  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)\n      \\le N\\cdot\\frac{3}{3}=N,\n\\]\n\nwith strict inequality because the three smallest proper divisors cannot all equal \\(3\\).  \nHence the sequence strictly decreases until an even term appears.\n\n*Let an even term \\(E\\) appear.*  \nDenote its three smallest proper divisors (greater than\u202f1) by \\(d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\\).  \nFour mutually exclusive possibilities occur.\n\n1. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,4\\).**  \n   Then \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{4}=13/12>1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   f(E)=E\\cdot\\frac{13}{12}=13\\cdot\\frac{E}{12}=13k .\n   \\]\n\n   The integer \\(13k\\) has at most two proper divisors greater than\u202f1 unless \\(k\\) itself has at least two distinct proper divisors.  \n   In any case the next term is either a prime (if \\(k=1\\)) or of the form \\(13p\\) with \\(p\\) prime; such a number possesses exactly three proper divisors \\(\\{1,13,p\\}\\).  \n   Its three largest proper divisors are \\(13,p,1\\), whose sum equals \\(13+p+1\\), a number having fewer than three proper divisors.  \n   Hence the hypothesis fails.\n\n2. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,5\\)** (i.e. \\(5\\mid E\\) but \\(4\\nmid E\\)).  \n   Here \\(\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{5}=31/30>1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   f(E)=E\\cdot\\frac{31}{30}=31\\cdot\\frac{E}{30}=31k .\n   \\]\n\n   The same reasoning as in (1) shows that the subsequent term cannot have three proper divisors, contradicting the requirement.\n\n3. **\\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}=2,3,6\\).**  \n   This is precisely the fixed\u2011point situation described in \u00a72; the sequence becomes constant and satisfies all conditions.\n\n4. **\\(d_{1}=2\\) but \\(3\\nmid E\\).**  \n   Then the three smallest proper divisors are at most \\(2,4,5\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1{2}+\\frac1{4}+\\frac1{5}<1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(f(E)<E\\). The sequence decreases again, eventually reaching a term whose smallest proper divisor is \\(\\ge3\\); we return to the first case. Thus a term with fewer than three proper divisors must appear unless we are in case\u202f3.\n\nSince any initial integer not of the form \\(2\\cdot3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\) (with the stated restrictions on \\(m\\)) inevitably leads to one of the contradictions above, such integers cannot generate an infinite sequence satisfying the problem\u2019s hypothesis.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Final answer  \n\nThe admissible initial values are exactly the fixed points found in \u00a72:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{\\alpha}\\cdot m\\quad(\\alpha\\ge 1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\n\\text{all prime divisors of }m\\ge 7)} .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant (\\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\)), and every term has at least three proper divisors. No other starting integer can satisfy the conditions."
